me of the accounts that we had  that explained that people felt  re-traumatized after their  engagement with the criminal justice  system  was one that was difficult for us to to  to hear because  uh clearly that is not the intention  and we had to listen to that and find  a way of creating a system that would  not do that do you feel that rape has  been  effectively decriminalized in the uk  because of the low conviction rates  uh i don't think that's something that  was within review  groups remit or that i can comment on  it's a crime that's taken very seriously  and it continues to be taken very  seriously and  this review shows how seriously we  intend to take it  okay thank you so much and thank you so  much for coming on and explaining some  of the details about the review as well  lady dorian there well let's get some  reaction to that interview and the  review  we're joined now by caroline noakes the  chair of the women and equalities  committee at westminster thank you for  being with us  um we just heard there some of the  details of lady dorian's review into  making the  whole justice system easier for victims  of rape and serious sexual offences  would you like to see some of these  changes apply  throughout the uk well i think it's  important to reflect that scotland has a  separate  justice system but it's absolutely  crucial that we find  effective ways to support victims  through the criminal justice  system whether they be male or female  and  there have been good initiatives put in  place the independent sexual violence  advisors who are supporting complainants  through the whole process and being with  them  in court but i think there are some  really significant problems  about encouraging victims to come  forward making sure that police make  referrals to the cps  making sure that the cps has the  confidence to take things to trial that  we see  not just more convictions but more  prosecutions you won't get the  convictions  without the prosecutions in the first  place and i really think it's time that  the government look closely at having  a women's justice strategy so that we  can see  victims supported from the start to the  end of the process  yeah as you say there are some clear  issues with the process not enough women  coming forward  not enough referring to the cps and then  if you look at the last three years rape  conviction is more than halving  in england and wales i mean one of the  most controversial suggestions from lady  dorian is the fact that actually some of  these trials  should be heard not by juries who may be  vulnerable to  rape myths for example but by trained  judges  i mean is that the kind of road that we  need to go down if we're going to get  serious about getting convictions  well we have a long history and  principle  of crimes being tried by a jury of your  peers in this country  and i think that that is important but i  also think it's right that we have a  full and frank conversation about how we  can do things better how perhaps we can  do things quicker i thought  one of the most important points that  lady dorian made was about  victims having to relive their trauma  potentially  many times and over a long period and i  think that we need to find a way that  the whole process can be speeded up so  that victims see cases coming  to court much more quickly and then have  the opportunity  to move on with the rest of their lives  it does feel doesn't it that one of the  positive  things after a really grueling couple of  weeks for so many women has been  that we are starting to have a more full  and frank conversation about the kind of  harassment and the kind of assault as  well that some people face  and so many women do see as a fabric of  their lives you  recently gave an interview where you  said a labour mp tried to grape you  i mean talk about your own experience if  you want to obviously don't feel that  you have to  but how much of an issue do you think  this is